= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <forward problem> evaluates a known model at a state: given $u$ and $K$, compute $f=Ku$. The associated <inverse problem> takes the data $f$ and the model $K$ and seeks a state $u$ with $Ku=f$.

In the sense of <Hadamard well-posedness>, a problem is well posed if a solution exists for every admissible datum, is unique, and depends continuously on the datum in the specified input and output topologies. An <inverse problem> is <ill-posed> if at least one of these requirements fails. Thus **ill-posedness means failure of existence, uniqueness or stability**. The topologies and the admissible data set matter: restricting the data to the range restores existence, but need not restore continuous dependence.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <bounded linear operator> $K:U\to V$ between <Hilbert spaces>, a <least-squares solution> of data $f$ is a minimizer of the residual <norm>:
$$
\bar u\in\arg\min_{u\in U}\|Ku-f\|_V^2.
$$
The <minimum-norm least-squares solution> is the one of smallest $U$-norm among these minimizers. Equivalently it is the unique <least-squares solution> in $\mathcal N(K)^\perp$. If the equation is consistent, this is the smallest-norm exact solution.

Let $P$ be the <orthogonal projection> onto $\overline{\mathcal R(K)}$. The <orthogonal decomposition by a closed subspace> gives
$$
\|Ku-f\|^2=\|Ku-Pf\|^2+\|(I-P)f\|^2.
$$
The first term has infimum zero by the definition of closure; that infimum is attained exactly if $Pf\in\mathcal R(K)$. This proves the <least-squares existence criterion>:
$$
\boxed{\text{least-squares solutions exist}\iff f\in\mathcal R(K)\oplus\mathcal R(K)^\perp.}
$$
They exist for every datum precisely when the range is closed. Once a solution exists, all solutions differ by elements of the closed <null space>, and projecting onto its <orthogonal complement> gives the unique minimum-norm solution.

For a counterexample to existence, take $K:\ell^2\to\ell^2$, $(Ku)_j=u_j/j$, and $f_j=1/j$. The range is dense, so the least residual infimum is zero; finite truncations realize residuals tending to zero. But an exact preimage would have every coordinate equal to one and would not belong to $\ell^2$. Thus no least-squares minimizer exists for this datum.

